

Power of RAG answering the Q lies with how good the chunking is.

Why is it called "Character Splitting"?

	Character is atomic, indivisible unit of text. You cannot split a single character in half.
	The name does not mean you are splitting individual characters apart. Instead, it means characters are the units being used to 
	calculate where the split happens.
		• What it means: 
			It is short for "Splitting a document based on character counts."
		• The Contrast: 
			It explicitly distinguishes it from Word Splitting (counting spaces), Sentence Splitting (counting periods), 
		  	or Token Splitting (counting model-specific sub-word fragments).


Types of chunking:
	1. Character splitting
		AKA (Fixed-Size Chunking)
		A foundational text-splitting strategy that divides a long document into smaller segments based on a strict, 
		pre-determined count of individual characters (including letters, numbers, spaces, and punctuation), rather than 
		syntax, sentences, or semantic meaning.

		Character Splitting counts literal string characters (len(text)). It is often confused with Token Splitting, which 
		counts words/sub-words based on a specific model's tokenizer (like OpenAI or Nomic). Because 1 token usually equals 
		roughly 4 characters in English, a Character Splitter set to 500 will yield much smaller snippets than a Token Splitter 
		set to 500.

		Chunk Size: The maximum number of characters allowed in a single chunk (e.g., 500 characters).
		Chunk Overlap: A specified number of characters duplicated between consecutive chunks (e.g., 50 characters). This 
			       creates a "sliding window" to ensure words or context cut off at the boundary are preserved in the next chunk.

	2. Recursive character splitting
		Definition:
			An advanced, structure-aware evolution of character splitting. It attempts to maintain text chunks at a target 
			character size, but prioritizes cutting text at natural grammatical boundaries (like paragraphs and sentences) 
			rather than blindly chopping words in half mid-letter.

		Core Mechanics:
			Instead of using a single hard character limit, it uses a hierarchical list of separators (ordered from largest 
			structural unit to smallest). The default order in standard libraries (like LangChain) is typically.

		1. "\n\n" (Paragraphs)
		2. "\n" (Lines/Sentences)
		3. " " (Words)
		4. "" (Individual characters — the absolute fallback)

	
		How It Works (Under the Hood):
			It uses a fallback look-ahead logic to split the text:
			1. Try Paragraphs: 
				It looks at the document and tries to split it by double newlines (\n\n). If the resulting paragraphs are 
				smaller than your target chunk size, it keeps them intact.
			2. Fallback to Lines: 
				If a single paragraph is still larger than the target chunk size, it moves down the list and tries to split 
				that specific paragraph by single newlines (\n).
			3. Fallback to Words: 
				If a line is still too big, it splits it by spaces ( ).
			4. Ultimate Fallback: 
				Only if a single word is larger than your chunk size will it finally resort to a hard cut mid-word using 
				empty characters ("").

	3. Document based chunking
		Definition
			A chunking strategy that completely ignores arbitrary character or token limits. Instead, it parses the underlying 
			layout, syntax, or format of a specific document type to split text natively at structural boundaries (such as 
			Markdown headers, JSON keys, HTML tags, or PDF sections).

		Core Mechanics
			• Syntax-Driven:
				The splitter relies on the document’s code or structural markers (e.g., #, ## in Markdown, or <tr>, 
			  	<td> in HTML).
			• Logical Boundaries:
				Each chunk is inherently designed to represent a complete, standalone concept or section planned by the 
				document's original author.
		
		How It Works (Under the Hood)
			Unlike character splitters that count strings, a Document-Based Splitter operates like a basic code parser:
				1. It reads the file and detects structural markers (e.g., a Markdown split on # Header 1).
				2. It groups all the text underneath that specific header into a single chunk.
				3. When it hits the next equivalent header (# Header 2), it cleanly closes Chunk 1 and starts Chunk 2.
				4. Metadata Enrichment: It automatically attaches the source header name or structural path 
				   (e.g., {"section": "Introduction"}) to the chunk's metadata, making it highly searchable for retrievers.


	4. Semantic chunking with embeddings [uses Embeddings]
		Definition
			An advanced, dynamic chunking strategy that completely ditches fixed character limits and syntax tags. Instead, it 
			uses vector embeddings and mathematical distance to calculate exactly where the meaning of a text changes, slicing 
			the document only when a new topic or idea begins.

		Core Mechanics
			• Sentence-Level Core: The document is first broken down into individual, raw sentences.
			• Semantic Distance / Similarity: An embedding model evaluates the vector space distance between consecutive sentences
			  (or sliding groups of sentences).
			• Dynamic Breakpoints: A mathematical threshold (like a sudden drop in cosine similarity) determines where a chunk 
			  ends and a new one begins.

		How It Works (Under the Hood)

			1. Sentence Splitting: The text is divided into individual sentences.
			2. Buffer/Windowing: To capture context smoothly, it often groups a sliding window of sentences together 
			   (e.g., combining sentences 1, 2, 3) before embedding them.
			3. Embedding Generation: It passes these sentence groups through an embedding model (like your nomic-embed-text) to 
			   generate vectors.
			4. Calculate Distance: It calculates the semantic distance (e.g., Cosine Distance) between the vector of group A and 
			   group B.
			5. Threshold Trigger: It charts these distances across the whole document. Wherever there is a massive statistical 
			   spike in distance (meaning the topic changed abruptly), it drops a chunk boundary marker.

	5. Segmenting chunking [uses LLM]
		Definition
			An intelligent text-splitting strategy that leaves algorithmic rules entirely behind. Instead of relying on character 
			counts, punctuation, or vector math, it uses a generative model (LLM) or a specialized layout engine to analyze the 
			document as a human reader would, segmenting the text into highly tailored, contextual chapters, topics, or semantic 
			units.

		Core Mechanics
			• Model-Driven: 
				Uses an LLM or an specialized multi-modal layout model (like LayoutLM) to read the source text.
			• Intentional Breakpoints: 
				The segments are determined by identifying shifts in narrative focus, transitions in instructional steps, or 
				distinct changes in a conversational thread.
	
		How It Works (Under the Hood)
			There are two primary ways this is executed in modern RAG engineering:
				1. The LLM-as-a-Parser Approach: 
					The raw text is streamed to a fast, cost-effective LLM with a prompt instruction: "Read this text and 
					insert a special marker [SPLIT] whenever the topic shifts or a new instructional step begins." The 
					system then splits the string precisely at those custom markers.
				2. The Layout Segmenter Approach: 
					For complex documents (like PDFs or financial reports), an object-detection model identifies the 
					physical structure of the page—separating sidebars, callout boxes, headers, and footnotes into 
					independent visual and structural segments before pulling the text out.


